so when we're thinking about the ideas  for this talk we were kind of looking at  customers who are trying to isolate  things within within that cluster and so  typical scenario is you have some sort  of trusted workload some sort of  untrusted workload the examples we use  on the talk our payment system is the  trusted side and a company that is doing  sort of web hosting has a container  that's provided by a customer and that  that's basically untrusted code from a  security perspective and so people want  to like to separate these things out so  you could use a cluster boundary but a  lot of people are actually looking at  kind of putting the the trusted thing on  trusted nodes and the untrusted thing on  untrusted nodes so using a VM and a node  effectively to separate those those two  kinds of workloads and that is a pretty  comfortable place for security people  they are used to separating things with  VMs but in Kupa days it's actually a  little bit different there's actually  quite a lot of connections between nodes  and a lot of privilege that's held by  nodes that makes that isolation just  probably not as good as a lot of people  think it is and so we really wanted to  highlight that in this talk and give a  practical demonstration of kind of how  to break that isolation and sort of talk  about some alternatives  you wanna talk about kind of like node  versus pod and what the alternatives are  there yeah sure  so the node is really complicated  there's a lot of stuff going on and the  attacker an attacker who has compromised  a node has access to the union of all of  those different things that the node is  using they have a pretty large toolkit  even if you're running least privileged  and so we kind of compare what the  attacker has access to in the node with  what they have access to in the pod  which it you can scope down much more  and this slide kind of summarizes the  differences there and then we wrap up by  looking at what you can actually do to  defend in addition to or instead of node  isolation before the attacker can get to  actually leveraging all of those  privileges at the node level they first  have to go through your application  getting remote code execution they then  need to break out of the container and  only then can they get access to the  whole node so first we recommend  hardening the application then we look  at different ways of pardoning the  container and we talked about sandboxes  like gke sandboxes and deep with divisor  as a way of hardening that pod boundary  a lot more and then we see that at that  point node isolation isn't as critical  so we finish off by talking about how  nodes are really complicated and  shouldn't be your only measure of  defense and instead we recommend using  container isolation and pod sandboxing  as a much more secure form of isolation  so I hope you enjoyed the talk and we'll  be hanging out the Google booth so  please come by and talk to us if you  wanna talk about it  charity and GA and isolation or anything  else Thanks  [Music]
 

